use-prefix
import
	../../html
	../../page-template

tutorial-index html[]()
	- section
		- p quote
			In this section, we'll run this "hello world" program using different methods.
		- crow-runnable quote
			main void()
				info log "Hello, world!"
		- h2 "Run in the browser"
		- p quote
			Click the {crow-icon "play"} button at the bottom of the code block above to run it in the browser.
			{br}
			The code is editable, so you can test things out there while reading the tutorial.
			{br}
			This supports all Crow standard library functions, but it can't do I/O except for printing.

		- h2 "Run from the command line"
		- p quote
			For the next step, you must {link "download.html", "download"} and install crow.
			{br}
			Copy the example above and save to a file {code "hello.crow"}.
			{br}
			Then, run {code "crow hello.crow"}.
		- p quote
			The longer form is {code "crow run hello.crow"}. This allows passing additional arguments like {code "--watch"}.

		- h3 "Script arguments"
		- p quote
			If you use {code "crow hello.crow foo"}, {code "foo"} becomes the first command-line argument to the script.
			{br}
			If you use {code "crow run hello.crow foo"}, {code "foo"} is treated as an argument to {code "crow"} instead (producing an error).
			{br}
			To work around this, command-line arguments to {code "crow run"} should come after a {code "--"}, as in {code "crow run hello.crow -- foo"}.

		- h3 "Watch mode"
		- p quote
			You can also use {code "crow run hello.crow --watch"} to re-run the script every time it (or its imports) changes.

		- h3 "Build"
		- p quote
			Run {code "crow build hello.crow"} to generate an executable JAR file.
			{br}
			You can then run {code "./hello"} to run it.
			{br}
			On some systems you must run {code "java -jar hello"}.
		- p quote
			You can also run {code "crow build hello.crow --out hello.js"} to generate JavaScript.
			{br}
			The result can be loaded as a script tag in a browser using {code "<script type=\"module\" src=\"/hello.js\"></script>"}.
			{br}
			It can also be run with {code "node hello.js"}. This isn't really an intended use case, but may be more convenient for testing.
		- p "{"crow build hello.crow --watch"} will rebuild the executable every time something changes."

		- h3 "Compile errors don't stop the program"
		- p quote
			For convenience, compile errors don't always prevent the program from starting.
			This allows you to use the parts of your program that don't have compile errors.
			{br}
			Instead, they compile to code that throws an exception.
			The below example will log {code "hello, world"}, then throw an exception:
		- crow-runnable quote
			main void()
				info log "hello, world"
				function-that-does-not-exist
				info log "goodbye, world"
